        "source": "Healing Injuries",
        "description": "<h3 id=\"healing-injuries\"><em>Healing Injuries</em></h3>\n<p>When performing first-aid on yourself or someone else, you can attempt to turn a Major Wound into a Minor Wound. <strong>Roll + Mind</strong>. On a 7-9, the wound is not healed, but you manage to stop bleeding from a non-crippling wound. On a 6 or lower, the target loses one point of consciousness, and bleeding from a major cutting wound loses the limit on the amount of consciousness it can lower someone to, meaning it can be lethal. If two ones are rolled while attempting first-aid, the major wound becomes crippling. On a 10+, the major wound becomes a minor wound, and consciousness lost is restored. First-aid can be performed on a minor piercing wound, and on a 10+ it is replaced with a minor cutting wound.  <br />\n<strong>Minor wounds gained from a healed major wound should be marked as such on the character sheet.</strong>  <br />\nIf you are performing first-aid with bandages, you recieve a +1. Otherwise, you must use something like a piece of your uniform as a makeshift bandage to close a cut or set a bone. When performing first-aid with a full first-aid kit, it has 3 uses, and gives a <strong>+2 Advantage</strong> when used.  <br />\nMinor wounds will heal slowly on their own over time. When you’ve had a moment to rest, <strong>Roll + Body</strong>. On a 10+, one minor wound (Not piercing)  of the GM’s choosing can be removed. Minor wounds that are the result of a major wound being healed cannot be removed until your mission is over. Alternatively, instead of removing a minor wound, the GM can restore a point of consciousness lost through fighting, bleeding, or other factors not related to a major wound.  <br />\nCrippling wounds cannot be properly healed, however crippling cutting  can be mitigated. When performing first-aid on a crippling cutting wound, <strong>Roll + Mind</strong> with the same rules as above. On a 10+, the limb is still gone, however the bleeding has been stopped, and consciousness lost can be restored in a similar manner to a minor wound healing.</p>",
